course: dictionary
lesson: 3636
title: Finish: an end, a line, a shine
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Finish is a small word with a few meanings. Read the words around it to see which one fits. «Kieran finished his chips first» tells you he ate the last of them before anyone else. Here finish means to complete something, to reach the end.
explain: Finish can be a verb (to finish) or a noun (a finish). In a race, the finish is the end point, where the finish line is. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Finish can also mean using up the last of something, as in finish off your juice. «When Leah finished every puzzle, she checked each one step by step.» Here finished means completed. Look for clue words like race, line, chips or puzzle to find the right meaning.
fact: To finish means to come to the end of something, to complete it.
fact: The finish is the end of a race, where the finish line is.
fact: To finish something up or off means to use or eat the last of it.
fact: A finish is also the look of a surface, like a shiny finish.
fact: «Start to finish» means the whole way through, from beginning to end.
exercise: «Kieran finished his chips first.» What does finished mean here? | *Ate the last of them | Dropped them on the floor | Started cooking them | Lost them in a game
exercise: «By half-term Theo tells the whole class a joke, start to finish.» What does start to finish mean? | Just the funny bit at the end | Very loudly at the start | Only the middle part | *From the beginning to the end
exercise: In a race, what is the finish? | The water stop halfway round | A kind of shiny paint | The place where the race starts | *The end point, where the finish line is
test: «Please finish your homework before tea.» What does finish mean here? | To lose your pencil | To paint a shiny surface | *To complete it, to come to the end | To start something new
test: Mia says the table has a lovely shiny finish. What is a finish in this sentence? | *The look of a surface | The last bite of food | The end line of a race | A short prayer before eating
exam: Which sentence uses finish to mean use up the last of something? | «He could finish the book by Friday.» | «The race had a clear finish line.» | *«Finish your juice before you leave.» | «Her table had a smooth, shiny finish.»
